{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"The React docs page teaches using JavaScript's filter() and map() methods with React to filter and transform an array of data into an array of components.","source_span":"you'll use `filter()` and `map()` with React to filter and transform your array of data into an array of components"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"The example people array contains five entries: Creola Katherine Johnson (mathematician), Mario José Molina-Pasquel Henríquez (chemist), Mohammad Abdus Salam (physicist), Percy Lavon Julian (chemist), and Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar (astrophysicist).","source_span":"const people = [{\n  id: 0,\n  name: 'Creola Katherine Johnson',\n  profession: 'mathematician',\n}, {..."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"Rendering a list of items without a unique key prop produces the console warning: \"Each child in a list should have a unique 'key' prop.\"","source_span":"Warning: Each child in a list should have a unique \"key\" prop."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"filter() takes an array of items, passes each through a test function returning true or false, and returns a new array containing only the items that passed.","source_span":"This method takes an array of items, passes them through a \"test\" (a function that returns `true` or `false`), and returns a new array of only those items that passed the test"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"The test function used to filter the people array down to chemists is (person) => person.profession === 'chemist'.","source_span":"The \"test\" function for this looks like `(person) => person.profession === 'chemist'`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"Arrow functions implicitly return the expression right after =>, but you must write return explicitly if the => is followed by a curly brace {.","source_span":"you must write `return` explicitly if your `=>` is followed by a `{` curly brace!"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"Percy Lavon Julian's accomplishment is listed as pioneering cortisone drugs, steroids and birth control pills.","source_span":"accomplishment: 'pioneering cortisone drugs, steroids and birth control pills'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"Mario José Molina-Pasquel Henríquez's accomplishment is listed as discovery of the Arctic ozone hole.","source_span":"accomplishment: 'discovery of Arctic ozone hole'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","statement":"The getImageUrl function builds an image URL as 'https://react.dev/images/docs/scientists/' + person.imageId + 's.jpg'.","source_span":"'https://react.dev/images/docs/scientists/' +\n    person.imageId +\n    's.jpg'"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What console warning appears when rendering a list of items in React without unique keys?","answer":"React shows the warning: \"Each child in a list should have a unique 'key' prop.\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Which two JavaScript array methods does the React 'Rendering Lists' guide use to filter and transform data into components?","answer":"It uses filter() and map()."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[6],"question":"According to the example data, what is Percy Lavon Julian known for?","answer":"Pioneering cortisone drugs, steroids, and birth control pills."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What test function is used with filter() to get only the chemists from the people array?","answer":"(person) => person.profession === 'chemist'"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[5],"question":"When writing an arrow function for map(), when must you explicitly include a return statement?","answer":"When the arrow function's => is followed by a curly brace { — otherwise the return is implicit."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is Mario José Molina-Pasquel Henríquez's profession and accomplishment in the example dataset?","answer":"He is listed as a chemist, known for the discovery of the Arctic ozone hole."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_rendering-lists","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How does the example getImageUrl function construct a scientist's image URL?","answer":"It concatenates 'https://react.dev/images/docs/scientists/' with the person's imageId and 's.jpg'."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"React event handlers are functions triggered in response to interactions like clicking, hovering, and focusing form inputs.","source_span":"Event handlers are your own functions that will be triggered in response to interactions like clicking, hovering, focusing form inputs, and so on."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"To add an event handler, you define a function and pass it as a prop to the JSX tag, e.g. onClick={handleClick}.","source_span":"Add `onClick={handleClick}` to the `<button>` JSX."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"By convention, event handler functions are named 'handle' followed by the event name, such as handleClick or handleMouseEnter.","source_span":"it is common to name event handlers as `handle` followed by the event name. You'll often see `onClick={handleClick}`, `onMouseEnter={handleMouseEnter}`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"Writing onClick={handleClick()} (with parentheses) is incorrect because it calls the function immediately during rendering rather than passing it to fire on click.","source_span":"the `()` at the end of `handleClick()` fires the function *immediately* during [rendering], without any clicks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"Writing <button onClick={alert('You clicked me!')}> causes the alert to fire on every component render, not on click.","source_span":"This alert fires when the component renders, not when clicked!"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"Because event handlers are declared inside components, they have access to the component's props, as shown by AlertButton calling alert(message) using its message prop.","source_span":"Because event handlers are declared inside of a component, they have access to the component's props. Here is a button that, when clicked, shows an alert with its `message` prop"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","statement":"In the example, PlayButton passes handlePlayClick as the onClick prop to its child Button, and UploadButton passes () => alert('Uploading!') as the onClick prop to its child Button.","source_span":"`PlayButton` passes `handlePlayClick` as the `onClick` prop to the `Button` inside.\n- `UploadButton` passes `() => alert('Uploading!')` as the `onClick` prop to the `Button` inside."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why is writing <button onClick={handleClick()}> considered incorrect in React?","answer":"The parentheses cause handleClick to fire immediately during rendering, instead of being passed as a function to run only when the button is clicked."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What naming convention is commonly used for React event handler functions?","answer":"They are typically named 'handle' followed by the event name, e.g. handleClick or handleMouseEnter."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What happens if you write <button onClick={alert('You clicked me!')}> in JSX?","answer":"The alert fires every time the component renders, not when the button is actually clicked."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In the AlertButton example, how does the button access the text to display in its alert?","answer":"The event handler reads the component's message prop directly, since it's declared inside the component, e.g. onClick={() => alert(message)}."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In the Toolbar example, what onClick value does UploadButton pass down to its Button child?","answer":"UploadButton passes () => alert('Uploading!') as the onClick prop to Button."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_responding-to-events","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What kinds of interactions can trigger a React event handler?","answer":"Interactions like clicking, hovering, and focusing form inputs, among others."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"React comes with several built-in Hooks including useState, useContext, and useEffect.","source_span":"React comes with several built-in Hooks like `useState`, `useContext`, and `useEffect`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"Custom Hooks can be created for specific purposes such as fetching data, tracking whether the user is online, or connecting to a chat room.","source_span":"for example, to fetch data, to keep track of whether the user is online, or to connect to a chat room. You might not find these Hooks in React, but you can create your own Hooks"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"The custom useOnlineStatus Hook uses useState to track isOnline and useEffect to subscribe to the browser's 'online' and 'offline' window events.","source_span":"function useOnlineStatus() {\n  const [isOnline, setIsOnline] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    function handleOnline() {\n      setIsOnline(true);\n    }\n    function handleOffline() {\n      setIsOnline(false);\n    }\n    window.addEventListener('online', handleOnline);\n    window.addEventListener('offline', handleOffline);"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"The useOnlineStatus Hook returns the isOnline boolean value.","source_span":"At the end of the function, return `isOnline`. This lets your components read that value"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"Extracting the shared logic into useOnlineStatus removes the code duplication that previously existed between the StatusBar and SaveButton components.","source_span":"Although there is no such built-in Hook, you can write it yourself... move all the duplicated code into it from the components you wrote earlier"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"In the SaveButton example, the button becomes disabled and shows 'Reconnecting...' instead of 'Save' while the network is off.","source_span":"You want to implement a Save button that will become disabled and show \"Reconnecting...\" instead of \"Save\" while the network is off."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","statement":"Custom Hook names must always start with 'use'.","source_span":"### Hook names always start with `use`"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What two built-in Hooks does the custom useOnlineStatus Hook combine, and what value does it return?","answer":"It combines useState and useEffect, subscribing to the browser's 'online' and 'offline' events, and returns the isOnline boolean value."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Why did the article extract the useOnlineStatus custom Hook out of the StatusBar and SaveButton components?","answer":"Both components had duplicated the same useState/useEffect logic for tracking online status, so extracting a custom Hook removed that duplication."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[5]," question":"What does the SaveButton example do when the network connection is off?","answer":"It becomes disabled and shows 'Reconnecting...' instead of 'Save'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What naming rule must all custom Hooks follow according to this guide?","answer":"Custom Hook names must always start with 'use'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are three example purposes for which a custom Hook might be created?","answer":"Fetching data, tracking whether the user is online, or connecting to a chat room."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Which built-in Hooks does React come with, according to the introduction of this guide?","answer":"useState, useContext, and useEffect."}
